Session 1, 13.10.2026

Today I have been learning the basics, which were all new to me. We explored a code with Arduino, 
which made a light turn on in different time distances. 
We have used the microcontroller to connect to a board. The Coding with CC+ differs from python – 
and one very important step is to put a semikolon behind every command. 
This is the first time I have ever used such materials, so I had to figure out, how it works. 
The Breadboard is used for an electrical circuit, that is created through connecting the breadboard 
and the microcontroller with cables (+ and -). For the blinking lamp we have used an LED with 
plus and minus as well as a restricter to protect the LED lamp from too much voltage. For this specific 
scenario we have used this code:

void setup() {
  // put your setup code here, to run once:
  pinMode(13, OUTPUT);
}

void loop() {
  // put your main code here, to run repeatedly:
  digitalWrite(13, LOW);//0-0v, off
  delay(1000);//1 second

  digitalWrite(13, HIGH);//1-5v, on
  delay(1000);//1 second
}

We have created a loop to make the lamp blink and experimented with different times to turn the lamp on and off. 
For me it was for about 15, where I wasn't able to tell anymore that the lamp is even blinking. 
This was very interesting to learn about how power works and how an electrical cycle works.

